export const modernPerspective = [
  'A two-dimensional topological classification belongs to a specified occupied subspace of a specified Hamiltonian. Dimensionality, filling, bulk gap, spin treatment, spin–orbit coupling, magnetic order, structural model, and protecting symmetries are therefore part of the claim. Band inversion is a useful clue in some models, but it is not itself a topological invariant.',
  'The Fu–Kane parity formula is an efficient diagnostic only when inversion symmetry is present. Without inversion symmetry, or when a general gauge-independent numerical route is preferred, the Z₂ invariant can be obtained from the occupied subspace through Wilson-loop or hybrid-Wannier-center evolution rather than from parity labels at isolated momenta.',
  'Helical edge protection is a symmetry statement, not a promise that every finite sample has exactly zero measured resistance. For an isolated Kramers pair, time-reversal symmetry forbids the simplest elastic single-particle backscattering between time-reversed partners, while bulk carriers, extra edge channels, inelastic processes, interactions, magnetic perturbations, finite-size hybridization, and contacts can still affect transport.',
];

export const synthesis =
  'Chapter 27 builds two-dimensional Chern and Z₂ insulators from spin–orbit-coupled models and ties their bulk classes to boundary connectivity. Chapter 28 extends that construction to three-dimensional weak, strong, and gapless phases.';
---

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-1-sp2">
  <h2 id="section-27-1-sp2">27.1 Two Dimensions: sp² Models</h2>
  <p>
    Martin begins from the one-dimensional s–p model used for the Shockley transition and adds the second in-plane p orbital. A two-dimensional site with s, p<sub>x</sub>, and p<sub>y</sub> states therefore gives an sp² model with three orbital bands, or six bands after spin is included. This is the smallest chemically transparent setting in which orbital angular momentum and atomic spin–orbit coupling can be treated directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The p orbitals can be reorganized into the complex combinations (p<sub>x</sub> ± i p<sub>y</sub>)/√2. These carry opposite orbital angular momentum around the out-of-plane axis. That change of basis is central: selecting one circulation breaks time-reversal symmetry and leads naturally to a Chern insulator, whereas retaining the time-reversed pair and coupling it to spin leads toward a time-reversal-invariant topological insulator.
  </p>
  <aside class="callout" aria-label="From the one-dimensional model to a two-dimensional topological insulator">
    <p><strong>Model progression</strong></p>
    <p>1D s–p chain → gap closing and Shockley transition.</p>
    <p>2D s + p<sub>x</sub> + p<sub>y</sub> → orbital circulation and Chern blocks.</p>
    <p>add spin–orbit coupling + time reversal → Kramers pairs and Z₂ topology.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-2-chern">
  <h2 id="section-27-2-chern">27.2 Chern Insulator and Anomalous Quantum Hall Effect</h2>
  <p>
    The cleanest precursor is obtained by breaking time-reversal symmetry and isolating one spin channel together with one circulating p orbital. The low-energy problem then reduces to two bands, one s-like and one p-like. For Martin’s square-lattice example the Pauli-vector part of the Hamiltonian can be written
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">h</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>[</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>sin</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.4em"/><mi>v</mi><mi>sin</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.4em"/><msub><mi>Δ</mi><mi>sp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>cos</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf h(\mathbf k)=[v\sin k_y,\,v\sin k_x,\,\Delta_&#123;sp&#125;+V(\cos k_x+\cos k_y)].</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    As (k<sub>x</sub>, k<sub>y</sub>) spans the Brillouin zone, the unit vector <strong>h</strong>/|<strong>h</strong>| covers a surface in Pauli-matrix space. If that mapping wraps the singular origin, the occupied band carries a nonzero Chern number. Equivalently, the Berry phase winds as the transverse momentum is varied, exactly as in Chapter 25.
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>BZ</mi></msub><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.8em"/><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>xy</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;2\pi&#125;\int_&#123;\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;&#125;\Omega_z(\mathbf k)\,d^2k,\qquad \sigma_&#123;xy&#125;=C\frac&#123;e^2&#125;&#123;h&#125;.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Bulk–boundary correspondence requires chiral edge bands whose net direction is fixed by the Chern number. The same Hamiltonian geometry can be read as the Thouless pump of Chapter 26 by replacing k<sub>y</sub> with a cyclic parameter. In the two-dimensional crystal, hybrid Wannier-center flow instead records charge transfer between opposite edges as crystal momentum changes, leading to the anomalous quantum Hall effect without a net external magnetic field.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-3-spin-orbit">
  <h2 id="section-27-3-spin-orbit">27.3 Spin–Orbit Interaction and the Diagonal Approximation</h2>
  <p>
    A time-reversal-invariant topological insulator needs the spin degree of freedom. Atomic spin–orbit coupling ties spin to orbital motion through
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>SO</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">L</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_&#123;\mathrm&#123;SO&#125;&#125;=\zeta\,\mathbf L\cdot\mathbf S.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    If the spin-flip terms are temporarily neglected, spin along one axis is conserved. The problem then separates into two blocks that experience opposite orbital circulation. Each block resembles a Chern insulator, with Chern numbers of opposite sign, so their charge Hall conductivities cancel while their edge modes counterpropagate.
  </p>
  <p>
    This diagonal approximation is exact in an idealized strictly two-dimensional p<sub>x</sub>–p<sub>y</sub> subspace where the omitted couplings vanish. A real layer still has three-dimensional orbitals and spin, so p<sub>z</sub> admixture and spin-flip matrix elements generally couple the two blocks. Spin is then not an exact conserved label, even though time reversal continues to relate the counterpropagating partners. That coupling is what changes the classification from two independent integers to a Z₂ invariant.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-4-z2">
  <h2 id="section-27-4-z2">27.4 Topological Insulators and the Z₂ Topological Invariant</h2>
  <p>
    For spin-1/2 electrons, time reversal is represented by an antiunitary operator Θ with Θ² = −1. A time-reversal-symmetric crystal therefore satisfies
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>Θ</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi>Θ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.8em"/><msup><mi>Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta H(\mathbf k)\Theta^&#123;-1&#125;=H(-\mathbf k),\qquad \Theta^2=-1.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    At the time-reversal-invariant momenta (TRIM), k and −k differ only by a reciprocal lattice vector, so Kramers theorem enforces degenerate pairs. Away from those special momenta the partners can mix. The total Chern number of a time-reversal-symmetric occupied space is zero because Berry curvature at k and −k cancels, but the way the Kramers pairs reconnect between TRIM can still have two inequivalent global patterns.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those two patterns define the Z₂ index ν = 0 or 1. When inversion symmetry is also present, Fu and Kane showed that the index can be obtained from the inversion eigenvalues of occupied Kramers pairs at the four two-dimensional TRIM,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></munderover><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>occ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">Λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^\nu=\prod_&#123;a=1&#125;^4\prod_&#123;m=1&#125;^&#123;N_&#123;\mathrm&#123;occ&#125;&#125;/2&#125;\xi_&#123;2m&#125;(\mathbf\Lambda_a).</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This parity shortcut uses inversion symmetry; it is not the definition of Z₂ topology for a general crystal. Martin also emphasizes formulations in terms of the occupied Bloch functions and the flow of hybrid Wannier centers. In modern calculations that flow, equivalently a Wilson-loop spectrum of the occupied subspace, is a broadly useful route because it does not require assigning a stable energy-ordered label to each occupied band.
  </p>
  <p>
    The boundary picture gives the same even-versus-odd distinction. Between the two edge TRIM, an even number of Kramers-paired crossings can be rearranged and removed without changing the bulk class, whereas an odd connectivity cannot be eliminated while the bulk remains gapped and time-reversal symmetry is preserved. The protected object is the connectivity between bulk valence and conduction manifolds, not the precise energy of one edge state at one momentum.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-5-square-lattice">
  <h2 id="section-27-5-square-lattice">27.5 Example of a Topological Insulator on a Square Lattice</h2>
  <p>
    Martin next restores the full spin–orbit interaction in an s–p square-lattice model. Without spin–orbit coupling the relevant p bands meet at symmetry points. Turning on ζ splits the atomic-like p states and opens a complete gap. For the lower occupied manifold in the chosen parameter regime, the band characters are inverted near the zone center relative to the zone boundary, and the parity product gives a Z₂-odd phase.
  </p>
  <p>
    A finite strip displays Kramers-degenerate edge bands at the edge TRIM that disperse in opposite directions away from those points and connect the occupied and empty bulk manifolds. Their detailed dispersion changes with ζ and with surface conditions, while the odd connectivity survives as long as the relevant bulk gap and time-reversal symmetry survive.
  </p>
  <p>
    The upper bands in the same model illustrate an equally important point. As ζ is varied, an upper gap closes and reopens with normal ordering, after which no protected edge connection crosses that gap. Topological classification therefore depends on the chosen occupied subspace and filling. Saying that an entire material is simply “topological” without specifying the gap or occupied manifold can hide this dependence.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-6-chains-planes">
  <h2 id="section-27-6-chains-planes">27.6 From Chains to Planes: Example of a Topological Transition</h2>
  <p>
    The relation to Chapter 26 becomes explicit by starting from weakly coupled one-dimensional chains and increasing the transverse hopping until a genuine two-dimensional lattice is formed. At small interchain coupling, each boundary resembles the Shockley end of a chain and its localized state broadens into an edge band as transverse momentum is introduced.
  </p>
  <p>
    Increasing the coupling competes with the fixed spin–orbit splitting. At a critical coupling the two-dimensional bulk gap closes at a symmetry momentum. Beyond that point it reopens with different band connectivity, and the edge spectrum changes from termination-sensitive Shockley-like bands to an odd Kramers connectivity that must cross the reopened bulk gap.
  </p>
  <p>
    This example separates two notions of protection. No particular edge-state energy at a general momentum is fixed: a surface potential can move it substantially. What topology constrains is the global connection of edge bands between the time-reversal-invariant momenta. That connection cannot be removed without closing the bulk gap or breaking the protecting time-reversal symmetry.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-7-hgcdte">
  <h2 id="section-27-7-hgcdte">27.7 Hg/CdTe Quantum Well Structures</h2>
  <p>
    HgTe/CdTe quantum wells provided the first experimental realization discussed by Martin. Bulk CdTe has normal band order near Γ, while HgTe has an inverted ordering associated with strong spin–orbit coupling. Confinement in a HgTe well produces two low-energy subbands, conventionally labeled E1 and H1, whose relative order depends on the well width.
  </p>
  <p>
    At a critical width E1 and H1 cross and the two-dimensional gap closes. Thinner wells lie on the normal side; sufficiently wide wells recover the inverted order and form the quantum spin Hall phase with helical edge states. The well thickness therefore acts as a physically controllable parameter that drives exactly the gap-closing transition required by the topological argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    Martin’s comparison of electronic-structure calculations adds a methodological lesson. A semilocal functional can misplace the narrow s–p ordering in HgTe, while a more accurate treatment can restore the experimentally consistent ordering. A topological invariant may be calculated perfectly for an approximate Hamiltonian and still describe the wrong material phase if that Hamiltonian gives the wrong gap or orbital ordering. Band-gap and band-order accuracy are therefore part of validating a material-specific topology claim.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-8-graphene">
  <h2 id="section-27-8-graphene">27.8 Graphene and the Two-Site Model</h2>
  <p>
    Graphene supplies the second route to the two-dimensional topological-insulator idea. Its two-site honeycomb lattice produces π bands from p<sub>z</sub> orbitals that meet linearly at the inequivalent K and K′ points. Haldane had shown that complex hopping can gap these Dirac points into a Chern phase when time-reversal symmetry is broken. Kane and Mele recognized that spin–orbit coupling produces time-reversed Haldane-like tendencies for the two spin sectors and yields a Z₂ topological insulator when spin is treated consistently.
  </p>
  <p>
    The intrinsic spin–orbit gap in carbon is extremely small because a pure p<sub>z</sub> model has no first-order atomic spin–orbit matrix element inside that low-energy subspace. The effect arises through virtual mixing with the other p orbitals and is therefore weak. This contrasts with the p<sub>x</sub>–p<sub>y</sub> systems considered in the next section, where spin–orbit coupling acts directly in the low-energy manifold.
  </p>
  <p>
    Zigzag graphene also illustrates why an edge band alone is not a topology diagnostic. Even with zero spin–orbit coupling, the projected bulk gap supports a termination-dependent Shockley-like zigzag edge state over part of the edge Brillouin zone. Spin–orbit coupling opens the bulk Dirac gap and changes the boundary connectivity into the helical form required by the Z₂ phase. Ordinary dangling-bond/termination physics and topological edge connectivity can therefore coexist in the same edge spectrum.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-27-9-honeycomb">
  <h2 id="section-27-9-honeycomb">27.9 Honeycomb Lattice Model with Large Spin–Orbit Interaction</h2>
  <p>
    The weakness of graphene’s intrinsic gap is not a limitation of the honeycomb geometry itself. If bonding to a substrate pushes p<sub>z</sub>-derived bonding and antibonding states away from the Fermi level while leaving p<sub>x</sub> and p<sub>y</sub> states near it, atomic spin–orbit coupling acts within the active low-energy subspace at first order rather than only through virtual mixing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Martin uses a bismuth honeycomb layer on a substrate as the example. The heavy atomic species supplies strong spin–orbit coupling, while the substrate changes which orbitals participate near the gap. The broader lesson is that topology is controlled by the complete low-energy Hamiltonian: lattice geometry, orbital selection, spin–orbit strength, and substrate-induced hybridization all help determine whether the required inverted and gapped occupied subspace is realized.
  </p>
  <p>
    For prerequisite repair on Chern numbers, Kramers structure, Wilson loops, and Z₂ classification, see <a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry Phases and Electronic Topology</a>. Chapter 28 carries the same time-reversal classification into three dimensions.
  </p>
</section>
